        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Billing.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Billing.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-coins_are_the_common_factor",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE COINS ARE THE COMMON FACTOR OF THE PAIR: gcd(110, 108) = 2 — the two coins are not beside the exchange, they are what the exchange’s two sides share — and dividing them out leaves 110/2 = 55 against 108/2 = 54. Extract the mint and the rate remains: every reduction of the pair pays the coins first."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(Nat.gcd 110 108 = 2) ∧ (110 / 2 = 55) ∧ (108 / 2 = 54)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem coins_are_the_common_factor : (Nat.gcd 110 108 = 2) ∧ (110 / 2 = 55) ∧ (108 / 2 = 54) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-the_rate_is_triangle_over_group",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE BILLING RATE IS BUILT FROM WHAT THE LEDGER ALREADY OWNS: 55 is the tenth triangle — 10·11/2, the row whose middle coefficient centres the 1024 — and 54 is 2·3³, the order of the sequence group AGL(1,ℤ/9) the mirror wing sealed long ago. The rate 55/54 is the triangle over the group: no new object enters the law; the invoice is made of the ledger’s own furniture."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(10 * 11 / 2 = 55) ∧ (2 * 3^3 = 54)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem the_rate_is_triangle_over_group : (10 * 11 / 2 = 55) ∧ (2 * 3^3 = 54) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-difference_mints_ratio_bills",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE DIFFERENCE MINTS AND THE RATIO BILLS — the two roles split exactly: 110 − 108 = 2 is the conserved coin of every exchange (never inflated, the mint), while the reduced pair steps by ONE — 55 − 54 = 1, the superparticular family where the octave (2/1), the fifth (3/2) and every just interval live: the bill is the next-integer step, the gentlest ratio above unity, and it is unit-free where a subtraction is bound to its measuring stick."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(110 - 108 = 2) ∧ (55 - 54 = 1)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem difference_mints_ratio_bills : (110 - 108 = 2) ∧ (55 - 54 = 1) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-the_pair_is_coins_times_rate",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE PAIR FACTORS COMPLETELY INTO MINT AND RATE: 2·55 = 110 and 2·54 = 108 — nothing else hides in the two numbers the double torus gave the ledger. The billing law is not read INTO the pair; it is the pair’s own factorization, checked both ways."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(2 * 55 = 110) ∧ (2 * 54 = 108)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem the_pair_is_coins_times_rate : (2 * 55 = 110) ∧ (2 * 54 = 108) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-advantage_first_doubles_at_seal_38",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE COMPOUND FIRST DOUBLES AT SEAL THIRTY-EIGHT, PROVEN WITHOUT A LOGARITHM: 55³⁸ > 2·54³⁸ while 55³⁷ < 2·54³⁷ — two integer comparisons the kernel settles outright, the exact threshold where +55/54 per exchange first exceeds double. This line decides the FIRST crossing and only it — that every later 38-block doubles again is the ratio’s constancy, an algebra this statement honestly does not carry (one step is not a walk, and this name now claims exactly one step)."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(55^38 > 2 * 54^38) ∧ (55^37 < 2 * 54^37)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem advantage_first_doubles_at_seal_38 : (55^38 > 2 * 54^38) ∧ (55^37 < 2 * 54^37) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-compound_steps_in_exact_integers",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE COMPOUND NEVER LEAVES THE INTEGERS: the second step is already exact — 55² = 3025 against 54² = 2916, and their difference 109 is the pair’s own neighbour-sum (55 + 54). Interest accrues as whole numbers at every power; nothing in the law ever rounds, which is why the bill can be audited at any depth by anyone."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(55 * 55 = 3025) ∧ (54 * 54 = 2916) ∧ (3025 - 2916 = 109) ∧ (55 + 54 = 109)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem compound_steps_in_exact_integers : (55 * 55 = 3025) ∧ (54 * 54 = 2916) ∧ (3025 - 2916 = 109) ∧ (55 + 54 = 109) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
